(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-general-dermatology-i-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"General Dermatology I Quiz 2 — CMS I Exam 1","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Where do pemphigus vulgaris lesions typically begin, and in which populations is it more common?","choices":["On the scalp, and it is more common in children","In the axillae, and it is endemic in rural Brazil","In the mouth, and it is more common in Jewish populations and people of Mediterranean descent","On the lower legs in the gaiter region, and it is far more common in older men than women"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Pemphigus foliaceus by contrast is endemic in rural Brazil and rarely involves mucosa. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 128"},{"q":"Which three agents make up the medium to high potency tier?","choices":["Triamcinolone acetonide 0.1%, hydrocortisone 1% and clobetasol propionate 0.05%","Betamethasone valerate 0.025%, tazarotene 0.1% and adapalene 0.1% preparations","Triamcinolone acetonide 0.1%, betamethasone valerate 0.1% and betamethasone dipropionate 0.05%","Hydrocortisone 2.5%, betamethasone valerate 0.025% and clobetasol propionate 0.05%"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Betamethasone appears twice here, in two different salts. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"What is the atopic triad, and what proportion of patients have at least one of its components?","choices":["Eczema, psoriasis and seborrhoeic dermatitis — approximately 30%","Asthma, allergic rhinitis and chronic sinusitis — approximately 60%","Atopic dermatitis, asthma and allergic rhinitis — approximately 80%","Atopic dermatitis, urticaria and food allergy — approximately 50%"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"What causes irritant contact dermatitis, and how common is it relative to the allergic form?","choices":["Overgrowth of Malassezia, and it is the most common form","A response to chemicals or friction that disrupts the skin barrier, and it is the most common form of contact dermatitis","A delayed type IV hypersensitivity reaction, and it is the less common form","An immunoglobulin E-mediated immediate hypersensitivity reaction, and it is the most common form of contact dermatitis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — frequently occupational, as in healthcare and janitorial staff who wash their hands often. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"How are petechiae and purpura distinguished by size in this lecture?","choices":["Both are defined as deposits of blood greater than 1 cm","Petechiae are deposits of blood 1 to 2 mm; purpura are 4 mm or greater","Petechiae are under 3 mm and purpura are 3 mm to 1 cm","Petechiae are 4 mm or greater and purpura are 1 to 2 mm, the reverse of the usual"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and purpura is flagged as a medical emergency until proven otherwise. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What defines a wheal?","choices":["A deposit of blood in the skin measuring one to two millimetres across","A firm oedematous plaque produced by infiltration of fluid into the dermis","A fluid-filled lesion containing leukocytes","A fluid-filled lesion under one centimetre"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the fluid is in the tissue rather than in a cavity, which is what separates it from a vesicle. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which androgen is chiefly responsible for male-pattern hair loss?","choices":["Testosterone acting directly on the follicle","Oestradiol","Cortisol","Dihydrotestosterone"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — male-pattern hair loss is an androgen-dependent trait with a strong paternal influence on risk. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 145"},{"q":"What is the recommended bathing and moisturising routine for xerosis?","choices":["Long hot showers taken twice daily, followed by a light fragrance-free lotion applied only once the skin has completely dried","Twice-daily antibacterial soap over the whole body","Avoiding bathing altogether until the skin recovers","Short lukewarm showers with a gentle fragrance-free cleanser only where needed, then a thick ointment or cream within minutes of bathing"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Petrolatum, ceramides and humectants such as urea or lactic acid help, though keratolytics may sting fissured skin. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 153"},{"q":"Which patients with lichen planus are at increased risk of squamous cell carcinoma?","choices":["Those with genital and erosive oral disease","Those with cutaneous disease of the wrists and ankles only","Those with nail involvement","Those with hepatitis C"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — an important reason to identify and follow mucosal involvement. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 176"},{"q":"How does the distribution of atopic dermatitis change with age?","choices":["Infants: flexures and popliteal fossae. Children: extensor surfaces and scalp. Adults: cheeks, scalp and the extensor surfaces of the limbs","The distribution is the same at every age","Infants: palms and soles. Children: trunk. Adults: lower legs","Infants: cheeks, scalp and extensor surfaces. Children: antecubital and popliteal fossae, wrists and ankles. Adults: flexures, hands, neck, face and eyelids"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the shift from extensor to flexural is the pattern to hold. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"What pattern does the pityriasis rosea eruption follow, and over what course?","choices":["It follows a dermatome and resolves within two weeks","It follows flexural creases and persists for months","It appears on the extensor surfaces of the limbs and persists indefinitely unless it is treated with phototherapy","It follows cleavage lines on the trunk in a Christmas tree pattern, spreading top down over days and fading over 4 to 6 weeks"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The herald patch clears centrally leaving a cigarette-paper or collarette of scale. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 169"},{"q":"Which supplement must accompany methotrexate?","choices":["Iron","Folic acid","Vitamin B12","Vitamin D"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — supplementation is given with methotrexate in every case. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 163"},{"q":"Which finding is listed as an urgent warning sign in a patient with a rash?","choices":["Fever with a rapidly spreading, painful, blistering, purple or non-blanching rash","Pruritus that disturbs sleep","A rash that has been present for more than six weeks","A rash that improves steadily with over-the-counter emollients used alone twice daily"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, alongside airway swelling, skin peeling involving eyes, mouth or genitals, severe pain with pus or red streaking, and a new rash after starting a medication. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"A fluid-filled lesion greater than one centimetre is called what?","choices":["A nodule","A bulla","A vesicle","A pustule"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — a vesicle is the same lesion up to one centimetre. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What produces lichen simplex chronicus?","choices":["Autoantibodies to adhesion molecules","Overgrowth of Malassezia","Repeated rubbing and scratching triggered by itch, stress, neuropathic sensation or another dermatosis","An autoimmune attack that destroys both the hair follicle and its stem cell compartment"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — producing intensely pruritic, well-demarcated lichenified plaques with exaggerated skin markings. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 180"},{"q":"Which biopsy takes the whole lesion with deep and wide margins?","choices":["Punch biopsy","Shave biopsy","Incisional biopsy","Excisional biopsy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and it always needs stitches to close. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"What organism is implicated in seborrhoeic dermatitis, and who is most affected?","choices":["Infection with Staphylococcus aureus, most common in children","Reactivation of varicella zoster virus, most common in the elderly","Overgrowth of Malassezia, most common in males aged 20 to 50","Overgrowth of Candida, most common in infants"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — Malassezia is normal skin flora that overgrows in sebum-rich areas. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 93"},{"q":"Which feature distinguishes irritant from candidal diaper dermatitis?","choices":["Only candidal disease causes erythema","Irritant disease affects convex surfaces and spares the inguinal folds; candidal disease involves the folds with satellite papules or pustules","Irritant disease involves the folds and candidal disease spares them","Irritant disease produces satellite papules and pustules within the inguinal folds while candidal disease spares them entirely"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — fold involvement is the discriminator, and it is called essential in the lecture. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 107"},{"q":"What are the two primary clinical priorities in stasis dermatitis?","choices":["Distinguishing it from cellulitis, and determining whether compression therapy can be used safely","Identifying the causative organism by culture and selecting an appropriate antibiotic","Determining the allergen and arranging patch testing","Staging the ulcer and measuring its depth"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — both are named as the priorities. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 112"},{"q":"Which finding is pathognomonic for alopecia areata?","choices":["Receding frontotemporal hairline","Exclamation point hairs","Broken hairs close to the scalp with scaling","Diffuse thinning of the central scalp"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — thinner at the root and normal calibre at the top, because growth is changing in real time. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 138"},{"q":"Which topical corticosteroid should be chosen for a sensitive site such as the face or the genitals?","choices":["Clobetasol propionate, for a shorter course","Betamethasone dipropionate, at half strength","Triamcinolone acetonide, applied once daily","Hydrocortisone, or another low potency agent"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — potency is selected according to site as well as severity. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"When should herpes simplex virus polymerase chain reaction be ordered in atopic dermatitis?","choices":["For painful monomorphic erosions or systemic illness","For every flare, since secondary infection is common","Only when the patient has known herpes simplex","Only in adult-onset disease"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Culture is reserved instead for purulent, pustular or significantly crusted lesions. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"Which agents are used for sensitive areas such as the face and eyelids in atopic dermatitis?","choices":["High-potency topical corticosteroids applied to the face","Topical retinoids","Topical antifungals","Tacrolimus and pimecrolimus, which are calcineurin inhibitors"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — they avoid the atrophy risk of steroids at those sites. Crisaborole, a topical phosphodiesterase-4 inhibitor, is also used. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"How can atopic dermatitis appear differently across skin tones?","choices":["Identical on all skin tones, since the eruption is defined by its distribution","Violaceous on lighter skin, but hypopigmented on darker skin in every case","Angry and inflamed on lighter skin, but almost silvery on darker skin","Silvery on lighter skin, but angry and inflamed on darker skin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the underlying disease is the same but the visual signal is not. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"Which six features are used to translate a dermatological finding into words?","choices":["Onset, duration, severity, triggers, treatments tried and response","Location, laterality, symmetry, tenderness, temperature and texture","Primary morphology, secondary morphology, demarcation, colour, size and distribution","Primary morphology, colour and distribution only, since the rest are subjective"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Getting all six into a description is what lets another provider picture the lesion without seeing it. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What is the Auspitz sign?","choices":["Fine white lines on the surface of a plaque","Removal of scale produces bleeding","Lesions appear at sites of trauma","The top layers of skin slough when rubbed"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — a classic psoriasis finding, alongside Koebner's phenomenon. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 159"},{"q":"How is pityriasis rosea treated?","choices":["It is self-limited, so reassurance is the mainstay, with antihistamines or topical steroids used cautiously for pruritus","A tapering course of oral prednisone given over a period of two to three weeks from the outset of the eruption","Topical ketoconazole","Oral doxycycline for six weeks"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Ultraviolet B phototherapy or sunlight helps if begun in the first week; severe cases may be treated with acyclovir. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 171"},{"q":"Who most commonly develops nummular eczema?","choices":["Men, and more often adults over the age of 50, though it can occur at any age","Women in their twenties and thirties","Infants and young children","Adolescents in the weeks following a streptococcal throat infection"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It is likely caused by some degree of skin barrier dysfunction. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"Which examination approach is most reliable when erythema is difficult to appreciate on darker skin?","choices":["Palpation for warmth and oedema, with attention to distribution and secondary change","Wood's lamp examination of every affected area in a darkened room","Immediate skin biopsy of the affected area, since clinical diagnosis is unreliable","Potassium hydroxide preparation from the affected area in every patient"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — colour stops being the primary signal, so the other findings carry the diagnosis. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 114"},{"q":"Which barrier protein is most notably reduced in atopic dermatitis?","choices":["Filaggrin","Keratin","Collagen","Elastin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — reduced filaggrin production is the named epidermal-barrier defect, alongside immune dysregulation. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 51"}]};
